= İnönü Walks Out of the Party Assembly

While CHP was never under a dicta of İnönü through his leadership, with him even suffering defeats -like in the 1950 congress- and there even being calls for his resignation in 1965, the authority within the Republican People's Party has long revolved around him with his service during his leadership through Second World War, transition to democracy, struggles against coup attempts and many other political problems since taking over from Atatürk.

However, despite it all, it is becoming increasingly clear that the Second Man of the Republic, the man that saved our democracy in both the 60s and now by managing to keep the parliament from being shut down by the 12th March, can no longer expect unconditional support from the party, and this couldn't be any more apparent then at the CHP Party Assembly in Ankara.

A special congress was called in order in response to the growing strength of the Left of Centre movement, led by Bülent Ecevit. However, confusion arose regarding who should be invited to the congress.

İnönü wished for only party delegates from the previous congress to attend. On the other hand, Ecevit’s supporters wanted all newly elected delegates (many of which were sympathetic to the reformist movement) to attend. It became clear that this debate was not purely procedural, but was actually a fight for control of the party. And İnönü, having grown accustomed to be the primary heavy weight of the party, would not so easily cave in as a clash of influence between the 2 factions would emerge.

Although İnönü agreed that the special congress should be held, he announced that it would be held in Ankara on the 5th of May. However, reformists within the party wished for the Party Assembly to consider this issue and decide what should be discussed in the special congress. İnönü however, believed that the issue had already been decided. Arguments rose in Ankara, and eventually, unwilling to continue the debate any further, İnönü concluded the meeting at his own will and left with his supporters.

Currently, İnönü remains the leader and no substantial changes had occured. What can be seen clearly from this confrontation however is just how split the party is. With the question of whenever we decide to continue this meeting without him or end it ourselves becoming our primary point of discussion.

The man who helped create a multi-party democracy in Turkey now has his authority called into question by internal party members. And in this situation, perhaps leaving the room will no longer be enough...

Nevertheless, we must now make a decision; do we decide to accept İnönü's decree and end the meeting now, or insist in our decision and continue onward as CHP is a party of parliamentary structure, and its actions cannot be determined against its delegates. Which action we will take will either weaken or strengthen our delegate support at the cost or gain of İnönü's favor, and dissent of either political aisle of our party.

- @continue_assembly: The party belongs to its members, not its chairman!
- @end_assembly: Though we disagree, this is not a hill worth dying on.

